1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,378
A tourniquet works by squeezing an arm or a leg so

2
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:07,688
hard that the main artery inside is pressed shut.

3
00:00:07,688 --> 00:00:09,917
Blood can no longer flow past it.

4
00:00:10,367 --> 00:00:12,871
It's one of the simplest devices in medicine.

5
00:00:12,871 --> 00:00:15,487
And for most of the twentieth century, military

6
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:17,212
doctors were taught to fear it.

7
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:21,050
In 1916, a British army surgeon wrote that he was

8
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:24,507
inclined to think tourniquets were an invention of

9
00:00:24,507 --> 00:00:25,406
the Evil One.

10
00:00:26,006 --> 00:00:29,069
90 years later, in a Baghdad hospital, Army

11
00:00:29,069 --> 00:00:31,206
researchers counted who lived.

12
00:00:31,206 --> 00:00:34,625
Among casualties who needed a tourniquet, 87% of

13
00:00:34,625 --> 00:00:36,548
those who got one survived.

14
00:00:36,548 --> 00:00:39,895
Of the five who did not get one, none survived.

15
00:00:40,495 --> 00:00:43,794
This is how a strap and a stick went from feared, to

16
00:00:43,794 --> 00:00:45,951
standard gear for American troops.

17
00:00:45,951 --> 00:00:47,029
And how it works.

18
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:48,678
The tourniquet.

19
00:00:48,678 --> 00:00:50,669
From feared, to lifesaver.

20
00:00:53,269 --> 00:00:54,268
The idea is old.

21
00:00:54,268 --> 00:00:56,390
The name comes from a French verb.

22
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:59,978
Roman surgeons used narrow bronze straps.

23
00:00:59,978 --> 00:01:01,798
And the arguments began early.

24
00:01:01,798 --> 00:01:04,771
The Greek physician Galen criticized tourniquets,

25
00:01:04,771 --> 00:01:07,501
saying they forced more blood from the wound.

26
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:11,778
In 1674, a French army surgeon named Morel described

27
00:01:11,778 --> 00:01:14,943
a tourniquet used at the siege of Besançon.

28
00:01:15,393 --> 00:01:18,774
In 1718, the Paris surgeon Jean-Louis Petit built a

29
00:01:18,774 --> 00:01:21,824
screw device to tighten a strap around a limb.

30
00:01:21,824 --> 00:01:25,470
He coined the word tourniquet, from the French tourner.

31
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:26,000
To turn.

32
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:29,684
In 1873, the German surgeon Friedrich von Esmarch

33
00:01:29,684 --> 00:01:32,983
reported a rubber bandage wrapped around the limb,

34
00:01:32,983 --> 00:01:36,085
that shut the arteries and controlled bleeding.

35
00:01:36,535 --> 00:01:39,286
His first aid lectures, published in English in

36
00:01:39,286 --> 00:01:41,511
1883, described an improvised version.

37
00:01:41,511 --> 00:01:44,496
A handkerchief tied loosely around the limb, with a

38
00:01:44,496 --> 00:01:46,136
short stick pushed under it.

39
00:01:46,136 --> 00:01:49,414
A walking stick, a door key, even a sword in its sheath.

40
00:01:49,414 --> 00:01:51,990
The stick was then twisted, round and round.

41
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:55,833
That twisting stick is the same idea inside a modern

42
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:56,551
tourniquet.

43
00:01:56,551 --> 00:01:58,574
Today it's called the windlass.

44
00:01:59,274 --> 00:02:01,324
So why did doctors turn against it?

45
00:02:02,424 --> 00:02:04,473
The complaint was always the same.

46
00:02:04,473 --> 00:02:07,909
Troops used them too often, too loosely, or for too long.

47
00:02:07,909 --> 00:02:10,682
During the American Civil War, the Confederate

48
00:02:10,682 --> 00:02:13,756
surgeon Julian Chisholm noted that troops too often

49
00:02:13,756 --> 00:02:15,444
used tourniquets needlessly.

50
00:02:15,894 --> 00:02:19,310
In 1862, according to one review, the Union surgeon

51
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:22,727
Samuel Gross made the first recorded recommendation

52
00:02:22,727 --> 00:02:25,809
to issue a tourniquet-like device to soldiers.

53
00:02:25,809 --> 00:02:29,159
Critics pushed back, often over poor training, and

54
00:02:29,159 --> 00:02:31,035
slow transport to a surgeon.

55
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:34,232
Around the Second World War, the British surgeon

56
00:02:34,232 --> 00:02:35,834
Watson-Jones put it bluntly.

57
00:02:36,284 --> 00:02:39,100
More limbs have been lost by the use of tourniquets

58
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:40,259
than have been saved.

59
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:42,175
Not everyone agreed.

60
00:02:42,175 --> 00:02:45,400
Late in the same war, two Army doctors, Wolff and

61
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:48,361
Adkins, reported on about 200 wounded service

62
00:02:48,361 --> 00:02:50,204
members who had tourniquets.

63
00:02:50,204 --> 00:02:53,428
They reported no gangrene, clots, or nerve damage

64
00:02:53,428 --> 00:02:55,797
they could attribute to the devices.

65
00:02:55,797 --> 00:02:57,311
The fear stayed anyway.

66
00:02:57,761 --> 00:03:00,456
And the American army absorbed that fear.

67
00:03:00,456 --> 00:03:03,677
In the 1970s and early eighties, Army medics were

68
00:03:03,677 --> 00:03:06,898
taught that in almost all cases, amputation would

69
00:03:06,898 --> 00:03:08,344
follow tourniquet use.

70
00:03:08,344 --> 00:03:11,433
A standard military surgery text, Emergency War

71
00:03:11,433 --> 00:03:14,654
Surgery, said tourniquets were rarely needed, and

72
00:03:14,654 --> 00:03:17,809
should be used only when all other methods fail.

73
00:03:18,259 --> 00:03:20,788
But Vietnam told a different story.

74
00:03:20,788 --> 00:03:24,329
Bleeding from wounds to the arms and legs was the

75
00:03:24,329 --> 00:03:27,942
leading cause of potentially preventable deaths on

76
00:03:27,942 --> 00:03:29,098
the battlefield.

77
00:03:29,098 --> 00:03:32,421
One surgeon's study of those killed in action,

78
00:03:32,421 --> 00:03:36,034
published in 1970, estimated that roughly 2,600 of

79
00:03:36,034 --> 00:03:39,719
them, about 7.4%, might have been saved with better

80
00:03:39,719 --> 00:03:41,526
care before the hospital.

81
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:44,832
Bleeding from a limb is the kind of bleeding a

82
00:03:44,832 --> 00:03:46,008
tourniquet can stop.

83
00:03:46,708 --> 00:03:49,390
And the lesson came back in 1991.

84
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:54,103
In Operation Desert Storm, of 143 soldiers treated in U.S.

85
00:03:54,103 --> 00:03:57,434
Army hospitals in one report, three died.

86
00:03:57,434 --> 00:04:01,579
Each of those deaths involved bleeding from a limb.

87
00:04:02,179 --> 00:04:04,313
The turn came from special operations.

88
00:04:05,413 --> 00:04:08,361
Frank Butler was a former Navy SEAL platoon

89
00:04:08,361 --> 00:04:10,897
commander, who became an eye surgeon.

90
00:04:10,897 --> 00:04:14,256
Starting in 1993, he and his colleagues rethought

91
00:04:14,256 --> 00:04:16,792
care on the battlefield from scratch.

92
00:04:16,792 --> 00:04:20,083
In 1996, they published Tactical Combat Casualty

93
00:04:20,083 --> 00:04:22,756
Care, in the journal Military Medicine.

94
00:04:23,206 --> 00:04:24,667
It split care into phases.

95
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:25,565
Care under fire.

96
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:26,689
Tactical field care.

97
00:04:26,689 --> 00:04:27,531
And evacuation.

98
00:04:27,531 --> 00:04:30,340
And it put the tourniquet back at the front of the

99
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:33,036
line, for life-threatening bleeding from a limb.

100
00:04:33,486 --> 00:04:36,539
Then came the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.

101
00:04:36,539 --> 00:04:40,089
A study of 82 special operations deaths, from late

102
00:04:40,089 --> 00:04:43,496
2001 through 2004, judged 12 of them potentially

103
00:04:43,496 --> 00:04:44,277
survivable.

104
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:47,827
Some of those involved bleeding from a limb that a

105
00:04:47,827 --> 00:04:50,169
tourniquet could have controlled.

106
00:04:50,619 --> 00:04:54,099
Special operations units that adopted the new rules

107
00:04:54,099 --> 00:04:55,395
early kept records.

108
00:04:55,395 --> 00:04:58,807
One program documented 67 uses of tourniquets, and

109
00:04:58,807 --> 00:05:02,355
not a single limb lost to the tourniquet cutting off

110
00:05:02,355 --> 00:05:03,651
blood for too long.

111
00:05:04,101 --> 00:05:07,320
In 2003, the Army Surgeon General recommended a

112
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:10,882
tourniquet called the Combat Application Tourniquet,

113
00:05:10,882 --> 00:05:12,115
as standard issue.

114
00:05:12,565 --> 00:05:15,979
In July 2005, an Army news story said another new

115
00:05:15,979 --> 00:05:19,463
tourniquet, the SOF Tactical Tourniquet, was being

116
00:05:19,463 --> 00:05:23,295
issued to all soldiers serving in Iraq and Afghanistan.

117
00:05:23,295 --> 00:05:26,639
It was designed for one-handed use, so a soldier

118
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:29,077
could put it on himself if need be.

119
00:05:29,527 --> 00:05:33,592
In 2005 and 2006, tourniquet use spread rapidly

120
00:05:33,592 --> 00:05:36,273
across the whole U.S. military.

121
00:05:36,973 --> 00:05:38,062
But did it work?

122
00:05:38,062 --> 00:05:40,377
Army researchers decided to count.

123
00:05:41,477 --> 00:05:45,496
Army Colonel John Kragh and his colleagues ran a

124
00:05:45,496 --> 00:05:49,849
study at a combat support hospital in Baghdad, for 7

125
00:05:49,849 --> 00:05:51,105
months in 2006.

126
00:05:51,105 --> 00:05:55,626
It included 2,838 casualties with major limb injuries.

127
00:05:55,626 --> 00:05:58,556
232 of them had tourniquets put on.

128
00:05:59,006 --> 00:06:01,048
Timing was linked to survival.

129
00:06:01,048 --> 00:06:04,519
Among patients whose tourniquet went on before they

130
00:06:04,519 --> 00:06:07,445
reached the emergency department, 11% died.

131
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:11,120
When it went on in the emergency department, 24% died.

132
00:06:11,570 --> 00:06:13,645
And the old fear did not hold up.

133
00:06:13,645 --> 00:06:16,790
In that study, no amputations resulted solely from

134
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:17,733
tourniquet use.

135
00:06:17,733 --> 00:06:20,312
4 patients had a temporary nerve problem.

136
00:06:20,762 --> 00:06:24,106
Later, a review of 4,596 American battlefield

137
00:06:24,106 --> 00:06:27,822
deaths, from 2001 to 2011, found that 87% happened

138
00:06:27,822 --> 00:06:31,314
before the casualty reached a medical facility.

139
00:06:31,314 --> 00:06:34,956
Of the deaths before a medical facility that were

140
00:06:34,956 --> 00:06:38,523
judged potentially survivable, about nine in ten

141
00:06:38,523 --> 00:06:40,752
were associated with bleeding.

142
00:06:40,752 --> 00:06:44,320
Most of that bleeding was in the torso, or where

143
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,961
limbs meet the body, beyond a tourniquet's reach.

144
00:06:48,411 --> 00:06:52,193
And potentially preventable deaths from limb

145
00:06:52,193 --> 00:06:56,576
bleeding fell, from 7.8% of the combat deaths in an

146
00:06:56,576 --> 00:07:00,272
earlier study, to 2.6% in the later review.

147
00:07:00,272 --> 00:07:01,475
A drop of 67%.

148
00:07:01,925 --> 00:07:05,614
Based on Kragh's work, the number of lives saved by

149
00:07:05,614 --> 00:07:09,013
tourniquets in those wars has been estimated at

150
00:07:09,013 --> 00:07:10,749
between 1,000 and 2,000.

151
00:07:11,649 --> 00:07:14,305
So what exactly is happening, inside that strap?

152
00:07:15,405 --> 00:07:18,061
Your heart pushes blood out through the arteries

153
00:07:18,061 --> 00:07:18,891
under pressure.

154
00:07:18,891 --> 00:07:21,769
When a major artery in a limb is torn, that pressure

155
00:07:21,769 --> 00:07:23,097
keeps pushing blood out.

156
00:07:23,547 --> 00:07:26,592
A tourniquet goes around the limb, above the injury.

157
00:07:26,592 --> 00:07:29,402
Tightened enough, it squeezes the limb until the

158
00:07:29,402 --> 00:07:32,681
artery inside is pressed flat, and flow beyond it stops.

159
00:07:32,681 --> 00:07:34,848
Doctors call this arterial occlusion.

160
00:07:35,298 --> 00:07:38,061
The Combat Application Tourniquet adds a rod, the

161
00:07:38,061 --> 00:07:38,568
windlass.

162
00:07:38,568 --> 00:07:41,331
Twisting it tightens the strap further, until the

163
00:07:41,331 --> 00:07:42,176
artery is shut.

164
00:07:42,176 --> 00:07:45,108
Then the rod is locked in place, so it can't unwind.

165
00:07:45,558 --> 00:07:48,332
And there's a strap marked time, to record when the

166
00:07:48,332 --> 00:07:49,366
tourniquet went on.

167
00:07:49,816 --> 00:07:51,440
It takes real force.

168
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:55,662
One review cites pressures of 250 to 428 millimeters

169
00:07:55,662 --> 00:07:59,966
of mercury, to shut off the artery in an adult thigh.

170
00:08:00,416 --> 00:08:02,729
That's why the old complaint had a point.

171
00:08:02,729 --> 00:08:05,551
A tourniquet that's too loose can fail to stop the

172
00:08:05,551 --> 00:08:06,059
bleeding.

173
00:08:06,059 --> 00:08:08,711
The same review says the concept is simple, but

174
00:08:08,711 --> 00:08:11,306
technique matters, and usually needs training.

175
00:08:11,756 --> 00:08:13,718
So soldiers drill it, over and over.

176
00:08:14,168 --> 00:08:16,222
Air Force recruits learn it too.

177
00:08:16,222 --> 00:08:19,365
In 2020, Basic Military Training added a Tactical

178
00:08:19,365 --> 00:08:22,124
Combat Casualty Care course for all Airmen.

179
00:08:22,124 --> 00:08:25,653
Tourniquet application is one of the skills it teaches.

180
00:08:26,353 --> 00:08:28,739
Then the lessons of the battlefield came home.

181
00:08:29,839 --> 00:08:33,024
After the Sandy Hook school shooting in 2012, the

182
00:08:33,024 --> 00:08:36,794
surgeon Lenworth Jacobs brought together national experts.

183
00:08:36,794 --> 00:08:40,044
Their recommendations became known as the Hartford

184
00:08:40,044 --> 00:08:40,694
Consensus.

185
00:08:40,694 --> 00:08:43,489
The goal was to improve survival after mass

186
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:46,479
shootings and other mass violence, by training

187
00:08:46,479 --> 00:08:47,649
bystanders to act.

188
00:08:48,099 --> 00:08:51,227
Then, in 2013, came the Boston Marathon bombing.

189
00:08:51,227 --> 00:08:53,703
Tourniquets were used in the response.

190
00:08:53,703 --> 00:08:57,091
But a later study found that all 27 tourniquets used

191
00:08:57,091 --> 00:08:59,632
were makeshift, not commercial devices.

192
00:08:59,632 --> 00:09:02,889
Its lead author called them improvised, and rarely

193
00:09:02,889 --> 00:09:03,541
effective.

194
00:09:03,991 --> 00:09:07,392
In October 2015, the White House launched a campaign

195
00:09:07,392 --> 00:09:08,831
called Stop the Bleed.

196
00:09:08,831 --> 00:09:11,775
It is led by the American College of Surgeons

197
00:09:11,775 --> 00:09:15,046
Committee on Trauma, and it runs under a licensing

198
00:09:15,046 --> 00:09:17,727
agreement with the Department of Defense.

199
00:09:18,177 --> 00:09:21,195
The courses teach ordinary people what military

200
00:09:21,195 --> 00:09:23,443
medics learned. Students. Teachers.

201
00:09:23,443 --> 00:09:25,048
Even members of Congress.

202
00:09:25,048 --> 00:09:27,874
By late August 2019, the American College of

203
00:09:27,874 --> 00:09:31,598
Surgeons said more than 1 million people had been trained.

204
00:09:32,048 --> 00:09:33,895
This video is not that training.

205
00:09:33,895 --> 00:09:36,781
It explains the history, and how the device works.

206
00:09:36,781 --> 00:09:39,725
Hands-on courses are where people learn to use one.

207
00:09:40,425 --> 00:09:43,560
For most of a century, the tourniquet was a last

208
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:45,519
resort, blamed for lost limbs.

209
00:09:45,969 --> 00:09:49,666
Then special operations doctors rewrote the rules,

210
00:09:49,666 --> 00:09:53,364
Army researchers counted the survivors, and troops

211
00:09:53,364 --> 00:09:55,656
heading to war were issued one.

212
00:09:55,656 --> 00:09:58,909
The idea Esmarch drew in 1883, a strap and a

213
00:09:58,909 --> 00:10:02,532
twisting stick, is now in first aid kits far from

214
00:10:02,532 --> 00:10:03,716
any battlefield.

215
00:10:04,616 --> 00:10:06,986
Next time on KEPT ALIVE. Penicillin.

216
00:10:06,986 --> 00:10:08,698
The mold that went to war.

217
00:10:09,498 --> 00:10:10,639
For education only.

218
00:10:10,639 --> 00:10:12,260
This is not medical advice.

219
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:15,081
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
